5: /// <summary>
6: /// One posting line of a <see cref="JournalTemplate"/>, in traditional accounting layout: each line
7: /// carries both its Debit account and its Credit account, sharing one amount expression. A complex
8: /// entry (more than 2 accounts) is still multiple lines — one line per elementary Debit/Credit pair —
9: /// exactly like a real journal entry is broken down on paper.
10: ///
11: /// Each side resolves its account from exactly one of two sources, snapshotted at save time so the
12: /// posting engine never needs to re-resolve anything at posting time:
13: /// - A fixed account (<see cref="DebitAccountId"/> / <see cref="CreditAccountId"/>), picked directly
14: ///   from the MasterData chart of accounts.
15: /// - An accounting item (<see cref="DebitAccountingItemId"/> / <see cref="CreditAccountingItemId"/>),
16: ///   which already carries its own account — picking the item is enough, no manual account pick.
17: /// </summary>
